<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Jason Albert writes about fatherhood, artificial intelligence, and the questions worth asking."
>
  <article class="post-single">
    <header class="post-header">
      <h1 class="post-title">About</h1>
    </header>
    <div class="post-content">
      <h2>Unsupervised Leap</h2>
      <p><em>No labels. No loss function. Just thinking out loud.</em></p>
      <p>
        This is a blog by <strong>Jason Albert</strong> — dad, AI thinker, and occasional
        philosopher.
      </p>
      <p>Three things I can't stop thinking about:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Fatherhood</strong> — the original unsupervised leap problem. No training data.
          Constant distribution shift. Somehow it works.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Artificial Intelligence</strong> — what we're actually building, what it reveals
          about us, and why the hard questions aren't technical.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Philosophy</strong> — the old tools that still cut. Stoicism, mortality, meaning,
          the examined life.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These three don't always connect neatly. Sometimes they connect in ways I didn't expect.
        That's what this blog is for.
      </p>
      <hr />
      <p>
        <em>Unsupervised Leap</em> is a term from machine learning: a system that finds structure in
        data without labeled examples. It feels like the right metaphor for how we actually grow —
        not by following instructions, but by paying attention.
      </p>
      <p>
        If something here resonates, I'd love to hear from you. Find me on{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/yourusername" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank"
          >GitHub</a
        >.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
